window.__programmeIndexChunk("contexts",{"e677fc3b29d74258ee60efa89e8e4db0805b29e7e2d0398ddb88da6d90fef89e":{"markdown":"<a id=\"historical-step-1\"><\/a>\n### Compact quotient thickenings shrink to the identity coset\n\nFor $0\\ne e\\in\\operatorname{Proj}(D)$, the reduced spectrum absorbs\n$\\Gamma$:\n\n<a id=\"equation-q3\"><\/a>\n$$\n\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e)+\\Gamma\n=\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e).\n\\tag{Q3}\n$$\n\nIndeed, every nonzero fixed projection in $eMe$ is also a nonzero fixed\nprojection in $M$, so\n\n<a id=\"equation-q4\"><\/a>\n$$\n\\Gamma(\\alpha)\\subseteq\\Gamma(\\alpha^e).\n\\tag{Q4}\n$$\n\nApply the Connes-spectrum absorption law of\n[The dual-center kernel through central overlap](OA-FLOW-L115.md#oa-flow.connes.absorption) to $\\alpha^e$;\n(Q3) follows from (Q4).\n\nLet $U$ range over compact neighborhoods of $0$ in $H$, and set\n\n<a id=\"equation-q5\"><\/a>\n$$\nA_{e,U}=\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e)+U,\n\\qquad\nK_{e,U}=\\pi(A_{e,U}).\n\\tag{Q5}\n$$\n\nThe sets $A_{e,U}$ are closed: the sum of a closed set and a compact set is\nclosed in a Hausdorff topological group.  Equation (Q3) makes them\n$\\Gamma$-saturated, so\n\n<a id=\"equation-q6\"><\/a>\n$$\n\\pi^{-1}(K_{e,U})=A_{e,U}.\n\\tag{Q6}\n$$\n\nThe quotient topology and (Q6) make $K_{e,U}$ closed.  Moreover,\n\n<a id=\"equation-q7\"><\/a>\n$$\nK_{e,U}\n\\subseteq S+\\pi(U),\n\\tag{Q7}\n$$\n\nand the right side is compact by (Q2).  Hence every $K_{e,U}$ is compact.\n\nThe directed-thickening theorem in\n[Mutual corner approximation through a family of spectral bridges](OA-FLOW-L121.md#oa-flow.directed.thickening)\nshows that the $A_{e,U}$, and therefore the $K_{e,U}$, are downward directed\nunder inclusion.  Restricting to compact neighborhoods loses nothing: inside\nany prescribed neighborhood of zero there is a compact neighborhood small\nenough for the same refinement argument.\n\nTheir intersection is the identity coset.  First,\n$\\Gamma\\subseteq A_{e,U}$ for every pair $(e,U)$.  Conversely, if\n$p\\notin\\Gamma$, the central-corner formula for the Connes spectrum supplies\n$0\\ne e\\in\\operatorname{Proj}(D)$ with\n$p\\notin\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e)$.  Closedness of that spectrum supplies a\ncompact neighborhood $U$ of zero such that\n\n<a id=\"equation-q8\"><\/a>\n$$\np\\notin\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e)+U.\n\\tag{Q8}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n<a id=\"equation-q9\"><\/a>\n$$\n\\bigcap_{e,U}A_{e,U}=\\Gamma.\n\\tag{Q9}\n$$\n\nBecause every member is saturated, inverse images commute with this\nintersection without losing representatives:\n\n<a id=\"equation-q10\"><\/a>\n$$\n\\pi^{-1}\\!\\left(\\bigcap_{e,U}K_{e,U}\\right)\n=\\bigcap_{e,U}\\pi^{-1}(K_{e,U})\n=\\Gamma.\n\\tag{Q10}\n$$\n\nConsequently\n\n<a id=\"equation-q11\"><\/a>\n$$\n\\boxed{\\bigcap_{e,U}K_{e,U}=\\{0+\\Gamma\\}.}\n\\tag{Q11}\n$$\n\nThis saturation step is what justifies passing the infinite intersection to\nthe quotient.\n\n","html":"<h3>Compact quotient thickenings shrink to the identity coset<\/h3>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\ne e\\in\\operatorname{Proj}(D)\\)\">\\(0\\ne e\\in\\operatorname{Proj}(D)\\)<\/span>, the reduced spectrum absorbs\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>:<\/p>\n<p>&lt;a id=&quot;equation-q3&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e)+\\Gamma\n=\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e).\n\\tag{Q3}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e)+\\Gamma\n=\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e).\n\\tag{Q3}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Indeed, every nonzero fixed projection in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(eMe\\)\">\\(eMe\\)<\/span> is also a nonzero fixed\nprojection in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, so<\/p>\n<p>&lt;a id=&quot;equation-q4&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\Gamma(\\alpha)\\subseteq\\Gamma(\\alpha^e).\n\\tag{Q4}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\Gamma(\\alpha)\\subseteq\\Gamma(\\alpha^e).\n\\tag{Q4}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Apply the Connes-spectrum absorption law of\n<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L115.html#oa-flow.connes.absorption\">The dual-center kernel through central overlap<\/a> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha^e\\)\">\\(\\alpha^e\\)<\/span>;\n(Q3) follows from (Q4).<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span> range over compact neighborhoods of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\)\">\\(0\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>, and set<\/p>\n<p>&lt;a id=&quot;equation-q5&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nA_{e,U}=\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e)+U,\n\\qquad\nK_{e,U}=\\pi(A_{e,U}).\n\\tag{Q5}\n\\]\">\\[\nA_{e,U}=\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e)+U,\n\\qquad\nK_{e,U}=\\pi(A_{e,U}).\n\\tag{Q5}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_{e,U}\\)\">\\(A_{e,U}\\)<\/span> are closed: the sum of a closed set and a compact set is\nclosed in a Hausdorff topological group.  Equation (Q3) makes them\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\)<\/span>-saturated, so<\/p>\n<p>&lt;a id=&quot;equation-q6&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\pi^{-1}(K_{e,U})=A_{e,U}.\n\\tag{Q6}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\pi^{-1}(K_{e,U})=A_{e,U}.\n\\tag{Q6}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The quotient topology and (Q6) make <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_{e,U}\\)\">\\(K_{e,U}\\)<\/span> closed.  Moreover,<\/p>\n<p>&lt;a id=&quot;equation-q7&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nK_{e,U}\n\\subseteq S+\\pi(U),\n\\tag{Q7}\n\\]\">\\[\nK_{e,U}\n\\subseteq S+\\pi(U),\n\\tag{Q7}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>and the right side is compact by (Q2).  Hence every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_{e,U}\\)\">\\(K_{e,U}\\)<\/span> is compact.<\/p>\n<p>The directed-thickening theorem in\n<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L121.html#oa-flow.directed.thickening\">Mutual corner approximation through a family of spectral bridges<\/a>\nshows that the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_{e,U}\\)\">\\(A_{e,U}\\)<\/span>, and therefore the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K_{e,U}\\)\">\\(K_{e,U}\\)<\/span>, are downward directed\nunder inclusion.  Restricting to compact neighborhoods loses nothing: inside\nany prescribed neighborhood of zero there is a compact neighborhood small\nenough for the same refinement argument.<\/p>\n<p>Their intersection is the identity coset.  First,\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Gamma\\subseteq A_{e,U}\\)\">\\(\\Gamma\\subseteq A_{e,U}\\)<\/span> for every pair <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((e,U)\\)\">\\((e,U)\\)<\/span>.  Conversely, if\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\notin\\Gamma\\)\">\\(p\\notin\\Gamma\\)<\/span>, the central-corner formula for the Connes spectrum supplies\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\ne e\\in\\operatorname{Proj}(D)\\)\">\\(0\\ne e\\in\\operatorname{Proj}(D)\\)<\/span> with\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\notin\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e)\\)\">\\(p\\notin\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e)\\)<\/span>.  Closedness of that spectrum supplies a\ncompact neighborhood <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span> of zero such that<\/p>\n<p>&lt;a id=&quot;equation-q8&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\np\\notin\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e)+U.\n\\tag{Q8}\n\\]\">\\[\np\\notin\\operatorname{Sp}(\\alpha^e)+U.\n\\tag{Q8}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Thus<\/p>\n<p>&lt;a id=&quot;equation-q9&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\bigcap_{e,U}A_{e,U}=\\Gamma.\n\\tag{Q9}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\bigcap_{e,U}A_{e,U}=\\Gamma.\n\\tag{Q9}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Because every member is saturated, inverse images commute with this\nintersection without losing representatives:<\/p>\n<p>&lt;a id=&quot;equation-q10&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\pi^{-1}\\!\\left(\\bigcap_{e,U}K_{e,U}\\right)\n=\\bigcap_{e,U}\\pi^{-1}(K_{e,U})\n=\\Gamma.\n\\tag{Q10}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\pi^{-1}\\!\\left(\\bigcap_{e,U}K_{e,U}\\right)\n=\\bigcap_{e,U}\\pi^{-1}(K_{e,U})\n=\\Gamma.\n\\tag{Q10}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Consequently<\/p>\n<p>&lt;a id=&quot;equation-q11&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\boxed{\\bigcap_{e,U}K_{e,U}=\\{0+\\Gamma\\}.}\n\\tag{Q11}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\boxed{\\bigcap_{e,U}K_{e,U}=\\{0+\\Gamma\\}.}\n\\tag{Q11}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>This saturation step is what justifies passing the infinite intersection to\nthe quotient.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L126.md","start_line":252,"end_line":358,"recorded_range_hash_matched":false},"e6c904634d7b1280ba4d427d4f08ddeec0f34edb4435e7ded7c7e5600d3a5d9d":{"markdown":"<a id=\"cdec-8\"><\/a>\n## 8. Five diagnostics with complete solutions\n\n**A. Can a finite algebra absorb the infinite matrix factor?** Let \\(A\\ne0\\) have finite unit. Explain the obstruction to \\(A\\cong A\\,\\overline\\otimes\\,B(\\ell^2)\\), and make it visible when \\(A=M_d(\\mathbb C)\\).\n\n**Solution.** Let \\(S\\delta_j=\\delta_{j+1}\\) be the unilateral shift. In the tensor algebra,\n\\[\n (1\\otimes S)^*(1\\otimes S)=1,\\qquad\n (1\\otimes S)(1\\otimes S)^*=1-1\\otimes e_{00}<1.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.a}\n\\]\nAn isomorphism onto \\(A\\) would take this isometry to an equivalence of its unit with a strict subprojection, contradicting finiteness. In \\(M_d(\\mathbb C)\\), equality of initial and final ranks forces every isometry to be unitary. Two isometries with orthogonal range projections would require two disjoint \\(d\\)-dimensional ranges in a \\(d\\)-dimensional space. Thus the filling isometries used by absorption cannot exist in this finite case. This is a projection obstruction, and does not depend on a chosen scalar trace or on a faithful-state hypothesis.\n\n**B. Why does a rank-one corner recover type III in the reverse direction?** For an arbitrary nonzero von Neumann algebra \\(A\\), prove\n\\[\n A\\,\\overline\\otimes\\,B(\\ell^2)\\text{ is type III}\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad A\\text{ is type III}.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.b}\n\\]\n\n**Solution.** If \\(p\\in A\\) were a nonzero finite projection, then \\(q=p\\otimes e_{00}\\) would be nonzero and\n\\[\n q\\bigl(A\\,\\overline\\otimes\\,B(\\ell^2)\\bigr)q\n   =pAp\\otimes\\mathbb C e_{00}\\cong pAp.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.c}\n\\]\nThis equality holds for the full von Neumann tensor product: compress finite sums of elementary tensors and use normality of compression. To check finiteness in the ambient algebra, any partial isometry \\(v\\) with \\(v^*v=q\\) and \\(vv^*\\le q\\) satisfies \\(v=qvq\\), so belongs to that corner. Finiteness of \\(p\\) then gives \\(vv^*=q\\). Thus \\(q\\) is finite, contradicting type III of the tensor algebra. This proves the reverse implication without any cancellation theorem. For the forward implication, [PC8](OA-FLOW-PC.md#oa-flow.pc.8) makes the unit of a nonzero type III algebra properly infinite. The proved absorption isomorphism in [Sections 1–2](#cdec-1) identifies the tensor algebra with \\(A\\), which has no nonzero finite projection. Both arguments allow arbitrary centers and Hilbert dimension.\n\n**C. How is the trace normalized when the center is arbitrary?** Let \\(M=N^\\theta\\), let \\(f:M\\to A\\) be a chosen normal isomorphism, and let \\(\\kappa:C(M)\\to N\\) be the trace-preserving reconstruction of [L30](OA-FLOW-L30.md#l30-6). Show that \\(F=C(f)\\kappa^{-1}\\) preserves the specified trace without invoking uniqueness of traces up to a scalar.\n\n**Solution.** Write \\(\\tau_M^{\\mathrm{can}}\\) and \\(\\tau_A^{\\mathrm{can}}\\) for the canonical core traces. The actual normal core functor [CORE8](OA-FLOW-CORE.md#core-8) proves its trace identity by transporting every bounded spectral sandwich of the dual weight and taking the same extended-positive supremum. L30 proves the corresponding full-cone identity for \\(\\kappa\\). Therefore, for every \\(y\\in N_+\\),\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\tau_A^{\\mathrm{can}}(F(y))\n &=\\tau_M^{\\mathrm{can}}(\\kappa^{-1}(y))\\\\\n &=\\tau(y).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{CDEC.8.d}\n\\]\nThis includes infinite values and fixes the normalization on every central part. The same two maps intertwine the flows, and both have normal inverses. A scalar comparison would be insufficient in general: on \\(B(\\ell^2)\\oplus B(\\ell^2)\\), the traces \\(\\operatorname{Tr}\\oplus\\operatorname{Tr}\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Tr}\\oplus2\\operatorname{Tr}\\) are not scalar multiples. Testing on a rank-one projection in the first summand would force that scalar to be \\(1\\), while testing in the second would force it to be \\(2\\). Formula (CDEC.8.d) uses neither factorhood nor such a comparison.\n\n**D. Does uniqueness of the decomposition give a unique conjugating map?** Suppose two normal isomorphisms \\(F_1,F_2:N\\to N'\\) intertwine the flows and preserve the specified traces. State exactly when the uniqueness result forces them to agree, and exhibit the remaining ambiguity.\n\n**Solution.** Let \\(M=N^\\theta\\) and \\(M'=(N')^{\\theta'}\\). Each \\(F_i\\) restricts to a normal isomorphism \\(f_i:M\\to M'\\). The full correspondence in [L30, Section 6](OA-FLOW-L30.md#l30-6) is\n\\[\n F_i=\\kappa' C(f_i)\\kappa^{-1}.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.e}\n\\]\nThus \\(F_1=F_2\\) if and only if \\(f_1=f_2\\): one implication is restriction, and the other follows from the displayed formula. Without the specified fixed-algebra map, a unitary \\(u\\in M\\) with \\(u\\notin Z(N)\\) gives distinct maps \\(F_1\\) and \\(F_1\\operatorname{Ad}(u)\\), both preserving trace and flow by (CDEC.7.h). There is no contradiction with uniqueness after fixing \\(f_i\\). Conversely, dropping trace preservation leaves the maps \\(F_1\\theta_a\\), which have the same restriction to \\(M\\), intertwine the flows, and satisfy\n\\[\n \\tau'\\circ F_1\\theta_a=e^{-a}\\tau.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.f}\n\\]\nFor \\(a\\ne0\\), the trace identity distinguishes them from \\(F_1\\). The two omissions therefore create different freedoms.\n\n**E. Does trace scaling alone force type III output?** Analyze the specified system\n\\[\n N=L^\\infty(\\mathbb R,dr),\\qquad\n (\\theta_sf)(r)=f(r+s),\\qquad\n \\tau(f)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^rf(r)\\,dr.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.g}\n\\]\n\n**Solution.** This is the complete scalar model in [L30, Section 8](OA-FLOW-L30.md#l30-8). The interval projections give a strongly dense finite trace domain, the density is positive, and nonnegative change of variables gives \\(\\tau\\theta_s=e^{-s}\\tau\\) on the entire positive cone. The action is point-ultraweakly continuous. Its full fixed algebra is \\(\\mathbb C1\\), by the translation-fixed multiplier proof used there. With \\(\\psi(1)=1\\), the normalized action integral and its density are\n\\[\n E(f)=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R}f(r)\\,dr,\n \\qquad H_\\psi(r)=\\frac{e^{-r}}{2\\pi}.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.h}\n\\]\nThe exact normal second-crossing formula [L30, Section 7](OA-FLOW-L30.md#l30-7) consequently gives\n\\[\n N\\rtimes_\\theta\\mathbb R\n   \\cong\\mathbb C\\,\\overline\\otimes\\,B(L^2(\\mathbb R))\n   =B(L^2(\\mathbb R)).\n \\tag{CDEC.8.i}\n\\]\nThis output is semifinite and properly infinite, with nonzero finite rank-one projections, so it is not type III. In particular this example does not satisfy the type III output hypothesis of the continuous-decomposition assertion. The precise additional restriction is [L18's supported central translation criterion](OA-FLOW-L18.md#l18-6). Here the whole center \\(N\\) itself is such a translation algebra. It supplies the semifinite output, despite the exact trace-scaling law.\n\n","html":"<h2>8. Five diagnostics with complete solutions<\/h2>\n<p><strong>A. Can a finite algebra absorb the infinite matrix factor?<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\ne0\\)\">\\(A\\ne0\\)<\/span> have finite unit. Explain the obstruction to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\cong A\\,\\overline\\otimes\\,B(\\ell^2)\\)\">\\(A\\cong A\\,\\overline\\otimes\\,B(\\ell^2)\\)<\/span>, and make it visible when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=M_d(\\mathbb C)\\)\">\\(A=M_d(\\mathbb C)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\delta_j=\\delta_{j+1}\\)\">\\(S\\delta_j=\\delta_{j+1}\\)<\/span> be the unilateral shift. In the tensor algebra,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (1\\otimes S)^*(1\\otimes S)=1,\\qquad\n (1\\otimes S)(1\\otimes S)^*=1-1\\otimes e_{00}&lt;1.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.a}\n\\]\">\\[\n (1\\otimes S)^*(1\\otimes S)=1,\\qquad\n (1\\otimes S)(1\\otimes S)^*=1-1\\otimes e_{00}&lt;1.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.a}\n\\]<\/span>\nAn isomorphism onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> would take this isometry to an equivalence of its unit with a strict subprojection, contradicting finiteness. In <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_d(\\mathbb C)\\)\">\\(M_d(\\mathbb C)\\)<\/span>, equality of initial and final ranks forces every isometry to be unitary. Two isometries with orthogonal range projections would require two disjoint <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span>-dimensional ranges in a <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span>-dimensional space. Thus the filling isometries used by absorption cannot exist in this finite case. This is a projection obstruction, and does not depend on a chosen scalar trace or on a faithful-state hypothesis.<\/p>\n<p><strong>B. Why does a rank-one corner recover type III in the reverse direction?<\/strong> For an arbitrary nonzero von Neumann algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>, prove\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n A\\,\\overline\\otimes\\,B(\\ell^2)\\text{ is type III}\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad A\\text{ is type III}.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.b}\n\\]\">\\[\n A\\,\\overline\\otimes\\,B(\\ell^2)\\text{ is type III}\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad A\\text{ is type III}.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.b}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\in A\\)\">\\(p\\in A\\)<\/span> were a nonzero finite projection, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q=p\\otimes e_{00}\\)\">\\(q=p\\otimes e_{00}\\)<\/span> would be nonzero and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n q\\bigl(A\\,\\overline\\otimes\\,B(\\ell^2)\\bigr)q\n   =pAp\\otimes\\mathbb C e_{00}\\cong pAp.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.c}\n\\]\">\\[\n q\\bigl(A\\,\\overline\\otimes\\,B(\\ell^2)\\bigr)q\n   =pAp\\otimes\\mathbb C e_{00}\\cong pAp.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.c}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis equality holds for the full von Neumann tensor product: compress finite sums of elementary tensors and use normality of compression. To check finiteness in the ambient algebra, any partial isometry <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v^*v=q\\)\">\\(v^*v=q\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(vv^*\\le q\\)\">\\(vv^*\\le q\\)<\/span> satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v=qvq\\)\">\\(v=qvq\\)<\/span>, so belongs to that corner. Finiteness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> then gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(vv^*=q\\)\">\\(vv^*=q\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\)\">\\(q\\)<\/span> is finite, contradicting type III of the tensor algebra. This proves the reverse implication without any cancellation theorem. For the forward implication, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PC.html#oa-flow.pc.8\">PC8<\/a> makes the unit of a nonzero type III algebra properly infinite. The proved absorption isomorphism in <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#cdec-1\">Sections 1–2<\/a> identifies the tensor algebra with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>, which has no nonzero finite projection. Both arguments allow arbitrary centers and Hilbert dimension.<\/p>\n<p><strong>C. How is the trace normalized when the center is arbitrary?<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=N^\\theta\\)\">\\(M=N^\\theta\\)<\/span>, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f:M\\to A\\)\">\\(f:M\\to A\\)<\/span> be a chosen normal isomorphism, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\kappa:C(M)\\to N\\)\">\\(\\kappa:C(M)\\to N\\)<\/span> be the trace-preserving reconstruction of <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L30.html#l30-6\">L30<\/a>. Show that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F=C(f)\\kappa^{-1}\\)\">\\(F=C(f)\\kappa^{-1}\\)<\/span> preserves the specified trace without invoking uniqueness of traces up to a scalar.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau_M^{\\mathrm{can}}\\)\">\\(\\tau_M^{\\mathrm{can}}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau_A^{\\mathrm{can}}\\)\">\\(\\tau_A^{\\mathrm{can}}\\)<\/span> for the canonical core traces. The actual normal core functor <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CORE.html#core-8\">CORE8<\/a> proves its trace identity by transporting every bounded spectral sandwich of the dual weight and taking the same extended-positive supremum. L30 proves the corresponding full-cone identity for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\kappa\\)\">\\(\\kappa\\)<\/span>. Therefore, for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\in N_+\\)\">\\(y\\in N_+\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\tau_A^{\\mathrm{can}}(F(y))\n &amp;=\\tau_M^{\\mathrm{can}}(\\kappa^{-1}(y))\\\\\n &amp;=\\tau(y).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{CDEC.8.d}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\tau_A^{\\mathrm{can}}(F(y))\n &amp;=\\tau_M^{\\mathrm{can}}(\\kappa^{-1}(y))\\\\\n &amp;=\\tau(y).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{CDEC.8.d}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis includes infinite values and fixes the normalization on every central part. The same two maps intertwine the flows, and both have normal inverses. A scalar comparison would be insufficient in general: on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(\\ell^2)\\oplus B(\\ell^2)\\)\">\\(B(\\ell^2)\\oplus B(\\ell^2)\\)<\/span>, the traces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}\\oplus\\operatorname{Tr}\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}\\oplus\\operatorname{Tr}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Tr}\\oplus2\\operatorname{Tr}\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Tr}\\oplus2\\operatorname{Tr}\\)<\/span> are not scalar multiples. Testing on a rank-one projection in the first summand would force that scalar to be <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>, while testing in the second would force it to be <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2\\)\">\\(2\\)<\/span>. Formula (CDEC.8.d) uses neither factorhood nor such a comparison.<\/p>\n<p><strong>D. Does uniqueness of the decomposition give a unique conjugating map?<\/strong> Suppose two normal isomorphisms <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_1,F_2:N\\to N&#x27;\\)\">\\(F_1,F_2:N\\to N&#x27;\\)<\/span> intertwine the flows and preserve the specified traces. State exactly when the uniqueness result forces them to agree, and exhibit the remaining ambiguity.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=N^\\theta\\)\">\\(M=N^\\theta\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M&#x27;=(N&#x27;)^{\\theta&#x27;}\\)\">\\(M&#x27;=(N&#x27;)^{\\theta&#x27;}\\)<\/span>. Each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_i\\)\">\\(F_i\\)<\/span> restricts to a normal isomorphism <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f_i:M\\to M&#x27;\\)\">\\(f_i:M\\to M&#x27;\\)<\/span>. The full correspondence in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L30.html#l30-6\">L30, Section 6<\/a> is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n F_i=\\kappa&#x27; C(f_i)\\kappa^{-1}.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.e}\n\\]\">\\[\n F_i=\\kappa&#x27; C(f_i)\\kappa^{-1}.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.e}\n\\]<\/span>\nThus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_1=F_2\\)\">\\(F_1=F_2\\)<\/span> if and only if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f_1=f_2\\)\">\\(f_1=f_2\\)<\/span>: one implication is restriction, and the other follows from the displayed formula. Without the specified fixed-algebra map, a unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\in M\\)\">\\(u\\in M\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\notin Z(N)\\)\">\\(u\\notin Z(N)\\)<\/span> gives distinct maps <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_1\\)\">\\(F_1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_1\\operatorname{Ad}(u)\\)\">\\(F_1\\operatorname{Ad}(u)\\)<\/span>, both preserving trace and flow by (CDEC.7.h). There is no contradiction with uniqueness after fixing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f_i\\)\">\\(f_i\\)<\/span>. Conversely, dropping trace preservation leaves the maps <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_1\\theta_a\\)\">\\(F_1\\theta_a\\)<\/span>, which have the same restriction to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>, intertwine the flows, and satisfy\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\tau&#x27;\\circ F_1\\theta_a=e^{-a}\\tau.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.f}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\tau&#x27;\\circ F_1\\theta_a=e^{-a}\\tau.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.f}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\ne0\\)\">\\(a\\ne0\\)<\/span>, the trace identity distinguishes them from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_1\\)\">\\(F_1\\)<\/span>. The two omissions therefore create different freedoms.<\/p>\n<p><strong>E. Does trace scaling alone force type III output?<\/strong> Analyze the specified system\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n N=L^\\infty(\\mathbb R,dr),\\qquad\n (\\theta_sf)(r)=f(r+s),\\qquad\n \\tau(f)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^rf(r)\\,dr.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.g}\n\\]\">\\[\n N=L^\\infty(\\mathbb R,dr),\\qquad\n (\\theta_sf)(r)=f(r+s),\\qquad\n \\tau(f)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^rf(r)\\,dr.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.g}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution.<\/strong> This is the complete scalar model in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L30.html#l30-8\">L30, Section 8<\/a>. The interval projections give a strongly dense finite trace domain, the density is positive, and nonnegative change of variables gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\theta_s=e^{-s}\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\theta_s=e^{-s}\\tau\\)<\/span> on the entire positive cone. The action is point-ultraweakly continuous. Its full fixed algebra is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb C1\\)\">\\(\\mathbb C1\\)<\/span>, by the translation-fixed multiplier proof used there. With <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi(1)=1\\)\">\\(\\psi(1)=1\\)<\/span>, the normalized action integral and its density are\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n E(f)=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R}f(r)\\,dr,\n \\qquad H_\\psi(r)=\\frac{e^{-r}}{2\\pi}.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.h}\n\\]\">\\[\n E(f)=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R}f(r)\\,dr,\n \\qquad H_\\psi(r)=\\frac{e^{-r}}{2\\pi}.\n \\tag{CDEC.8.h}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe exact normal second-crossing formula <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L30.html#l30-7\">L30, Section 7<\/a> consequently gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n N\\rtimes_\\theta\\mathbb R\n   \\cong\\mathbb C\\,\\overline\\otimes\\,B(L^2(\\mathbb R))\n   =B(L^2(\\mathbb R)).\n \\tag{CDEC.8.i}\n\\]\">\\[\n N\\rtimes_\\theta\\mathbb R\n   \\cong\\mathbb C\\,\\overline\\otimes\\,B(L^2(\\mathbb R))\n   =B(L^2(\\mathbb R)).\n \\tag{CDEC.8.i}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis output is semifinite and properly infinite, with nonzero finite rank-one projections, so it is not type III. In particular this example does not satisfy the type III output hypothesis of the continuous-decomposition assertion. The precise additional restriction is <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L18.html#l18-6\">L18's supported central translation criterion<\/a>. Here the whole center <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> itself is such a translation algebra. It supplies the semifinite output, despite the exact trace-scaling law.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-CDEC.md","start_line":529,"end_line":606,"recorded_range_hash_matched":false},"e63034465ed810f9904597de5541f6a7d8279f83d0ea4cea23226f2fad7ef88e":{"markdown":"# The central functional classifies closed unitary orbits\n\n<a id=\"crng-setting\"><\/a>\n\nThe invariant is a positive normal functional on the center of the continuous core. It is not a central projection. Its mass retains the mass of the original functional, while the flow records scalar multiplication.\n\nEarlier complete proofs: [CINV.MASS](OA-FLOW-CINV.md#cinv-mass), [CINV.INVERSE](OA-FLOW-CINV.md#cinv-inverse), [CINV.COVARIANCE](OA-FLOW-CINV.md#cinv-covariance), [CINV.METRIC](OA-FLOW-CINV.md#cinv-metric), [CSAS.DISTANCE](OA-FLOW-CSAS.md#csas-distance), [CSAS.RANGE](OA-FLOW-CSAS.md#csas-range), [CEXP.CONVERGENCE](OA-FLOW-CEXP.md#cexp-convergence), [CAPP.III-ZERO](OA-FLOW-CAPP.md#capp-iii-zero), [CPER.MASSES](OA-FLOW-CPER.md#cper-masses), [CPER.RANGE](OA-FLOW-CPER.md#cper-range), [MIV.1](OA-FLOW-MIV.md#miv-1), [MIV.2](OA-FLOW-MIV.md#miv-2), [HC.7](OA-FLOW-HC.md#oa-flow.hc.7), [SORB.TYPE-I](OA-FLOW-SORB.md#orbit-type-i), [SORB.RANGE](OA-FLOW-SORB.md#orbit-range), [CORE.9](OA-FLOW-CORE.md#core-9), [CSAS.TYPE-I](OA-FLOW-CSAS.md#csas-type-i), [CAPP.RESOLVENT](OA-FLOW-CAPP.md#capp-resolvent), [SORB.DIAMETERS](OA-FLOW-SORB.md#orbit-diameters).\n\n<a id=\"crng-theorem\"><\/a>\n## 1. The full theorem\n\nLet \\(M\\) be a nonzero infinite factor with separable predual. Let\n\\((C,\\tau,\\theta)\\) be its common continuous core, with original Haar measure \\(dt\\), dual Haar measure \\(ds/(2\\pi)\\), and \\(\\tau\\theta_s=e^{-s}\\tau\\). Put \\(Z=Z(C)\\). For every \\(\\phi\\in M_*^+\\), including zero and nonfaithful functionals, use SCW's supported dual density and define\n\\[\n e_\\phi=1_{(1,\\infty)}(h_\\phi),\\qquad\n \\chi_\\phi(z)=2\\pi\\tau(e_\\phi z)\\quad(z\\in Z).                      \\tag{OR1}\n\\]\nThen:\n\n1. The projection \\(e_\\phi\\) has trace \\(\\phi(1)/(2\\pi)\\), and\n   \\(\\chi_\\phi\\) is positive normal with \\(\\|\\chi_\\phi\\|=\\phi(1)\\).\n2. Canonical normal chart identifications carry this functional to the same invariant; \\(\\chi_{\\phi\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u}=\\chi_\\phi\\).\n3. For all positive normal \\(\\phi,\\psi\\),\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\quad\n   \\inf_{u\\in\\mathcal U(M)}\\|\\phi\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u-\\psi\\|\n          =\\|\\chi_\\phi-\\chi_\\psi\\| .\n   \\quad}                                                        \\tag{OR2}\n   \\]\n4. The map is a contraction for the predual norms, hence norm-continuous and Borel. For every \\(c>0\\),\n   \\[\n   \\chi_{c\\phi}=c\\,\\chi_\\phi\\circ\\theta_{\\log c}.                   \\tag{OR3}\n   \\]\n   At \\(c=0\\) its value is zero.\n5. If \\(M\\) is type II∞ or type III, its exact image is\n   \\[\n   P_\\theta=\\{\\chi\\in Z_*^+:\n               \\chi\\theta_s\\ge e^{-s}\\chi\\text{ for every }s\\ge0\\}.\n                                                                    \\tag{OR4}\n   \\]\n   For type I∞ the exact image is the discrete tail range in Section 3 below.\n6. The family \\(\\{\\chi_\\phi:\\phi\\in M_*^+\\}\\) separates \\(Z\\): an element of \\(Z\\) annihilated by every member is zero.\n\nThus (OR1) induces an onto isometry from norm-closed unitary orbits onto the stated image. Equality of invariants is exactly equality of those closed orbits. Restricting to mass one gives the corresponding normal-state theorem. The domain must be the positive predual for (OR3); nontrivial scalar multiplication does not preserve the state space.\n\n<a id=\"crng-cases\"><\/a>\n## 2. Proof of the distance and range in every type\n\n[The finite spectral-cut construction](OA-FLOW-CINV.md#cinv-mass) proves the mass, chart covariance, inner invariance, scalar covariance, contraction and membership in \\(P_\\theta\\), with the exact finite-domain pairings and full density domains. It also proves the metric on closed orbits is complete by a summable sequence of actual unitary alignments. Its support-sensitive partial-isometry approximation (CI18)–(CI19) retains zero complements and gives an explicit norm loss.\n\nFor type II∞, the [semifinite assembly](OA-FLOW-CSAS.md#csas-distance), including its [range construction](OA-FLOW-CSAS.md#csas-range), applies, with a one-point base in the factor case. Equations (SA12) and (SA18) prove (OR2) and (OR4). Its nonfactor theorem is needed below and was proved separately by measurable near-minimizers and a jointly selected full trace flag.\n\nFor type III\\(_\\lambda\\), \\(0<\\lambda<1\\), [the compact-to-continuous core comparison](OA-FLOW-CPER.md#cper-comparison) proves the normal spatial comparison between the compact and continuous cores, their exact trace scalar, the common supported densities and the actual central functional. Its unequal-mass extension (PCO13)–(PCO15) proves (OR2), and its full circle range (PCO16) proves (OR4). This use is a metric and range theorem; no diameter formula is substituted.\n\nFor type III₀, [the finite-orbit-block construction](OA-FLOW-CEXP.md#cexp-convergence) constructs increasing type II∞ subalgebras \\(F_i\\) of \\(M\\) with faithful normal compatible expectations satisfying\n\\(\\|\\eta E_i-\\eta\\|\\to0\\) for every \\(\\eta\\in M_*\\).\nThey are generally nonfactors. Their full distance and range statements are (SA12) and (SA18). [The core approximation argument](OA-FLOW-CAPP.md#capp-distance) lifts these expectations to the cores, proves exact trace preservation and supported-density inclusion, then proves the varying-center restriction-norm limit. Its equations (CM11) and (CM15) give (OR2) and (OR4) for the original type III₀ factor.\n\nFor completeness consider type III₁ explicitly. The actual modular-invariant theorem MIV1–2 identifies\n\\[\n S(M)\\cap(0,\\infty)=\\exp K,\\qquad\n K=\\ker(\\theta|_Z).\n\\]\nIts normal center comparison and ergodicity are part of that theorem. Since \\(S(M)=[0,\\infty)\\), injectivity of the real exponential gives \\(K=\\mathbb R\\). Thus the center action is trivial; ergodicity then gives \\(Z=\\mathbb C1\\). Formula (OR1) is therefore the scalar functional of mass \\(\\phi(1)\\).\n\nLet \\(m=\\phi(1)\\), \\(n=\\psi(1)\\). Testing on \\(1\\) gives the lower bound\n\\(\\delta_M(\\phi,\\psi)\\ge|m-n|\\). If one mass is zero, the bound is equality by the positive-functional norm identity. Otherwise write\n\\(\\phi=m\\rho,\\ \\psi=n\\sigma\\), with normal states \\(\\rho,\\sigma\\).\nThe full normal-state homogeneity theorem HC, including its nonfaithful approximation in HC7, gives unitaries with\n\\(\\|\\rho\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u-\\sigma\\|\\to0\\). Hence\n\\[\n \\|\\phi\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u-\\psi\\|\n \\le m\\|\\rho\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u-\\sigma\\|+|m-n|\n \\longrightarrow |m-n|.\n\\]\nThis proves\n\\[\n \\delta_M(\\phi,\\psi)=|m-n|=\\|\\chi_\\phi-\\chi_\\psi\\|.                 \\tag{OR5}\n\\]\nEvery nonnegative scalar is realized by a scalar multiple of one normal state. These are precisely the members of (OR4) on the scalar center.\n\n<a id=\"crng-type-i\"><\/a>\n## 3. The exact type I∞ range\n\nUse the usual trace on \\(B(H)\\), where \\(H\\) is separable and infinite-dimensional. In the Fourier coordinate of the continuous core, the central action is\n\\(\\theta_s(z)(q)=z(q-s)\\). If \\(\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2\\ge\\cdots\\ge0\\) are the positive eigenvalues of the trace-class density, repeated with multiplicity and padded by zeros as needed, then\n\\[\n f_\\phi(a)=\\#\\{j:\\lambda_j>a\\},\\qquad\n \\chi_\\phi(z)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}z(q)e^{-q}f_\\phi(e^{-q})\\,dq.         \\tag{OR6}\n\\]\nSORB's actual finite-eigenvector matching proof gives\n\\[\n \\delta_M(\\phi,\\psi)\n   =\\sum_j|\\lambda_j(\\phi)-\\lambda_j(\\psi)|\n   =\\int_0^\\infty|f_\\phi(a)-f_\\psi(a)|\\,da\n   =\\|\\chi_\\phi-\\chi_\\psi\\|.\n\\]\nThe exact range consists of decreasing right-continuous integrable\n\\(f:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb N_0\\) in (OR6). Conversely the eigenvalue formula\n\\(\\lambda_j=\\sup\\{a:f(a)\\ge j\\}\\) proves realization and\n\\(\\sum_j\\lambda_j=\\int f\\), as in SORB(SO26)–(SO27).\nIf the chosen semifinite trace is \\(\\alpha\\operatorname{Tr}\\), retain the corresponding value set \\(\\alpha\\mathbb N_0\\) and its trace-relative density; canonical chart covariance identifies the resulting core-center functional.\n\nThe continuous cone (OR4) has additional members. The profile\n\\(f(a)=\\tfrac12 1_{(0,2)}(a)\\) is decreasing, right-continuous, integrable with mass one, and gives a subinvariant functional by (OR6), but is not integer-valued. It is therefore absent from the type I∞ image. This is a range exception, not an exception to the isometry.\n\n<a id=\"crng-separation\"><\/a>\n## 4. Separation of the entire center\n\nFirst suppose \\(M\\) is type II∞ or III. [The resolvent-cone argument](OA-FLOW-CAPP.md#capp-resolvent), (CM12)–(CM13), proves that the positive resolvent averages\n\\[\n R\\eta=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-t}\\eta\\theta_{-t}\\,dt,\\qquad\\eta\\in Z_*^+,\n\\]\nbelong to \\(P_\\theta\\), and that their norm closure is \\(P_\\theta\\).\nLet \\(z\\in Z\\) satisfy \\(\\chi(z)=0\\) for every \\(\\chi\\in P_\\theta\\).\nSince the cone is invariant under every \\(\\theta_a\\), also\n\\((R\\eta)(\\theta_a z)=0\\) for all real \\(a\\). Write\n\\(h(a)=\\eta(\\theta_a z)\\); this is bounded and continuous. Changing variables gives\n\\[\n 0=e^a(R\\eta)(\\theta_a z)\n   =\\int_{-\\infty}^a e^r h(r)\\,dr\\qquad(a\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\]\nThe integrand is continuous and integrable at \\(-\\infty\\), so differentiation yields \\(e^ah(a)=0\\). In particular \\(\\eta(z)=0\\) for every positive normal \\(\\eta\\). Positive normal functionals separate \\(Z\\) (use vector functionals and polarization), hence \\(z=0\\). Full range (OR4) proves the asserted separation by the image.\n\nFor type I∞, use rank-one positive functionals of mass \\(a>0\\). Their profiles are \\(1_{(0,a)}\\). If \\(z\\) annihilates the whole image, then\n\\[\n \\int_t^\\infty e^{-q}z(q)\\,dq=0\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R),\n\\]\nby putting \\(a=e^{-t}\\). Differences show that the integral of \\(e^{-q}z(q)\\) on every finite interval is zero. Its locally integrable density is therefore zero almost everywhere, for example by uniqueness of the scalar Radon–Nikodym derivative. Thus \\(z=0\\) in this case as well.\n\nEquivalently, the norm-closed complex linear span of the image is \\(Z_*\\): if it were a proper closed subspace, Hahn–Banach and the concrete predual duality would supply a nonzero \\(z\\in Z\\) annihilating it. This is a separation statement about normal functionals, not an algebra-generation assertion about them.\n\n<a id=\"crng-checks\"><\/a>\n## 5. Exact consequences and projection coordinates\n\n- The zero orbit maps to zero, and distance to that orbit is exactly \\(\\phi(1)\\).\n- For \\(c=e^{-s}>0\\), equation (OR3) reads\n  \\(\\chi_{e^{-s}\\phi}=e^{-s}\\chi_\\phi\\theta_{-s}\\), with both the mass factor and negative time retained.\n- Equality of two invariants concerns closed orbits. It need not be attained by one unitary. Even on \\(B(\\ell^2)\\), the densities with diagonal lists \\((2^{-1},2^{-2},\\ldots)\\) and \\((0,2^{-1},2^{-2},\\ldots)\\) have the same positive eigenvalues but different kernel dimensions.\n- A scalar center has only two projections but has positive normal functionals of every nonnegative mass. The invariant in (OR1) therefore has the required codomain even in type III₁.\n\n<a id=\"crng-projection-coordinates\"><\/a>\n### Central projections as countable coordinates\n\nFix a faithful normal state \\(\\rho\\) on \\(Z\\). Such a state exists: the regular core embeds in \\(M\\bar\\otimes B(L^2(\\mathbb R))\\). The tensor-functional density argument in [CAPP, Section 2](OA-FLOW-CAPP.md#capp-lift), together with the separability of \\(M_*\\) and of the trace-class operators on \\(L^2(\\mathbb R)\\), makes the ambient predual separable. The normal restriction quotients in [CP6](OA-FLOW-CP.md#oa-flow.cp.6) give separability for \\(C_*\\) and \\(Z_*\\). The explicit faithful-state construction in [HC7](OA-FLOW-HC.md#oa-flow.hc.7) then applies.\n\nFor the required separable Hilbert realization, [CR8](OA-FLOW-CR.md#oa-flow.cr.8) sends a countable norm-dense set of positive normal functionals of \\(M\\) to a dense set in its standard cone, since \\(\\|\\xi_f-\\xi_g\\|^2\\le\\|f-g\\|\\). [CR1](OA-FLOW-CR.md#oa-flow.cr.1) proves that the complex span of that cone is the full standard Hilbert space. It is therefore separable, as is the regular core space \\(L^2(\\mathbb R,H)\\). In particular its represented center satisfies DC06's separable-Hilbert-space hypothesis.\n\nUse the abelian realization in [DC06](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DC.html#realizing-an-abelian-algebra-as-the-diagonal-algebra) and the finite-measure density proof in [DC05](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DC.html#a-finite-radon-nikodym-density-from-hilbert-representation). They give a unique nonnegative \\(g_\\phi\\in L^1(Z,\\rho)\\) with\n\\[\n \\chi_\\phi(z)=\\rho(g_\\phi z),\\qquad\n \\rho(g_\\phi)=\\phi(1),\\qquad\n \\|\\chi_\\phi-\\chi_\\psi\\|=\\rho(|g_\\phi-g_\\psi|).\n \\tag{OR7}\n\\]\nThe last equality follows by testing the bounded sign of the real density difference; the reverse inequality is the integral triangle inequality. Faithfulness of \\(\\rho\\) identifies its null sets with the null projections of \\(Z\\).\n\nFor every positive rational \\(r\\), define\n\\[\n p_{\\phi,r}=1_{(r,\\infty)}(g_\\phi)\\in\\operatorname{Proj}(Z),\\qquad\n \\mathbf p_\\rho(\\phi)=(p_{\\phi,r})_{r\\in\\mathbb Q_{>0}}.\n \\tag{OR8}\n\\]\nThe densities are finite almost everywhere, so their rational strict superlevel sets determine them. Explicitly, outside the union of the countably many null sets where two corresponding cuts differ, distinct finite density values would have a positive rational strictly between them. Thus the coordinate families coincide exactly when \\(g_\\phi=g_\\psi\\), equivalently \\(\\chi_\\phi=\\chi_\\psi\\), equivalently the norm-closed unitary orbits coincide by (OR2). This includes zero and all nonfaithful functionals.\n\nRepresent \\(Z\\) on \\(L^2(Z,\\rho)\\), and give its projection space the strong-operator Borel structure, metrized by \\(d_\\rho(p,q)=\\rho(|p-q|^2)^{1/2}\\). On uniformly bounded multiplication operators, convergence in \\(L^2(\\rho)\\) implies strong convergence: truncate any test vector to a bounded vector, use the \\(L^2\\) bound on that part, then use the common operator bound on the remaining vector tail. Conversely strong convergence tested on the constant vector gives \\(L^2\\) convergence. This also proves the asserted description on the projection space.\n\nEach map \\(\\phi\\mapsto p_{\\phi,r}\\) is Borel. Indeed (OR7) and contraction of \\(\\chi\\) make \\(\\phi\\mapsto g_\\phi\\) continuous from the positive predual norm into \\(L^1(\\rho)\\). Put\n\\[\n F_{n,r}(t)=\\min(1,n(t-r)_+).\n\\]\nFor nonnegative \\(g,h\\),\n\\(\\|F_{n,r}(g)-F_{n,r}(h)\\|_2^2\\le n\\|g-h\\|_1\\),\nsince the function has Lipschitz constant \\(n\\) and its values lie in \\([0,1]\\). Hence these bounded-operator maps are continuous. Dominated convergence gives \\(F_{n,r}(g)\\to1_{(r,\\infty)}(g)\\) strongly. A pointwise limit of Borel maps into the metric space of positive contractions with metric \\(d_\\rho\\) is Borel: distance to every closed set is the limit of the corresponding distances, and open sets can be tested by positive distance to their complements. The countable product map in (OR8) is consequently Borel.\n\nThe coordinate projections from the **full positive predual** generate \\(Z\\). Let \\(B\\) be the von Neumann algebra they generate. All \\(g_\\phi\\) are measurable for \\(B\\), because their rational spectral cuts lie in \\(B\\). Section 4 proves that the complex linear span of the \\(\\chi_\\phi\\) is norm dense in \\(Z_*\\); by (OR7), the same density holds for the span of the \\(g_\\phi\\) in \\(L^1(\\rho)\\). Given a selfadjoint \\(z\\in Z\\), \\(\\|z\\|\\le K\\), approximate it in \\(L^1\\) by real linear combinations of the \\(g_\\phi\\), taking real parts of complex approximations. Clip those combinations to \\([-K,K]\\). The clipped functions belong to \\(B\\), and clipping cannot increase their pointwise distance from \\(z\\). Thus they converge to \\(z\\) in \\(L^1\\), hence in \\(L^2\\) by the common bound, and strongly by the preceding vector-tail argument. Strong closedness gives \\(z\\in B\\), proving \\(B=Z\\).\n\n### Scalar covariance and the role of the domain\n\nFor real \\(s\\), let \\(d_s=d(\\rho\\theta_s)/d\\rho\\). It is positive and finite almost everywhere, because both states are faithful. For a nonnegative density \\(g\\), first use bounded cutoffs and then monotone convergence to obtain\n\\[\n (g\\rho)\\theta_s(z)=\\rho\\bigl(d_s\\,\\theta_{-s}(g)\\,z\\bigr)\n \\qquad(z\\in Z_+).\n\\]\nIndeed the right side is \\((\\rho\\theta_s)(\\theta_{-s}(g)z)=\\rho(g\\theta_s(z))\\). Formula (OR3) therefore becomes\n\\[\n g_{c\\phi}=c\\,d_{\\log c}\\,\\theta_{-\\log c}(g_\\phi),\\qquad\n p_{c\\phi,r}=1_{(r,\\infty)}\n       \\bigl(c\\,d_{\\log c}\\,\\theta_{-\\log c}(g_\\phi)\\bigr)\n \\quad(c>0).\n \\tag{OR9}\n\\]\nThe products are products of commuting measurable functions, and the displayed integral proves their integrability. At \\(c=0\\) every coordinate is zero. For a state \\(\\phi\\), the functional \\(e^{-s}\\phi\\) has mass \\(e^{-s}\\), so it belongs to the state space only when \\(s=0\\). The positive-predual domain and the density factor in (OR9) are therefore part of the statement.\n\nState inputs alone need not generate the center by these coordinates. For \\(M=B(\\ell^2)\\), a trace-class state density has every eigenvalue at most one. Formula (OR6) makes \\(\\chi_\\phi\\), and hence \\(g_\\phi\\), vanish on the half-line \\(q\\le0\\). All its positive spectral cuts vanish there. Their generated unital algebra restricts to scalars on that half-line, whereas \\(L^\\infty(\\mathbb R)\\) contains nonconstant functions supported there. Thus the generation theorem just proved genuinely requires the full positive domain.\n\nThe single spectral projection \\(e_\\phi\\) of (OR1) is a different object: it lies in \\(C\\) and need not be central or constant on a unitary orbit. In the same type I example, the vector state at a basis vector \\(e_j\\) has\n\\(e_\\phi=|e_j\\rangle\\langle e_j|\\otimes1_{(0,\\infty)}(q)\\).\nMatrix units show this projection is noncentral; exchanging two basis vectors changes it within one state orbit. The central functional in (OR1) is unchanged.\n\nFinally, no map into \\(\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\\) alone can separate all positive-functional orbits for every infinite factor. In type III₁, \\(Z=\\mathbb C\\) has only two projections, while (OR5) gives distinct orbits for any three distinct positive masses. The family (OR8) has the stated separation property. Its countable-coordinate assertion is distinct from a single-projection coding of the mass-one orbit space.\n\n<a id=\"crng-single-projection\"><\/a>\n### One central projection for each closed state orbit\n\nThere does exist a Borel map\n\\[\n p:S_*(M)\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\n \\tag{OR10}\n\\]\nsuch that \\(p(\\phi)=p(\\psi)\\) exactly when the closed state orbits coincide, and \\(p(S_*(M))\\) generates \\(Z\\). The following explicit coding proves existence. It is a choice of labels; its projections need not be the spectral projections in (OR1) or (OR8).\n\nFirst \\(Z\\) is either scalar or diffuse. [CORE8](OA-FLOW-CORE.md#core-8) proves \\(Z^\\theta=\\mathbb C1\\) because \\(M\\) is a factor. If \\(e\\) is a nonzero minimal projection of \\(Z\\), each \\(\\theta_s(e)\\) is either \\(e\\) or is orthogonal to \\(e\\). In the latter case\n\\(\\rho(|\\theta_s(e)-e|^2)\\ge\\rho(e)>0\\).\nContinuity makes \\(\\theta_s(e)=e\\) for all sufficiently small \\(s\\). Dividing any real \\(s\\) into sufficiently small equal increments proves invariance for every \\(s\\). Ergodicity gives \\(e=1\\), and minimality of \\(1\\) means \\(Z=\\mathbb C1\\). In this scalar case (OR2) makes all states have the same closed orbit, so \\(p(\\phi)=1\\) proves (OR10) and generation.\n\nSuppose now \\(Z\\) is diffuse. DC06 supplies a bounded positive selfadjoint generator \\(h\\) of \\(Z\\). Its spectral probability measure\n\\(\\mu(E)=\\rho(1_E(h))\\) has no atoms: a nonzero projection \\(1_{\\{a\\}}(h)\\) would be minimal in \\(Z=\\{h\\}''\\). Thus the distribution\n\\(F(a)=\\mu((-\\infty,a])\\) is continuous. For \\(0<t<1\\), put\n\\[\n q_t=\\inf\\{a:F(a)\\ge t\\},\\qquad P_t=1_{(-\\infty,q_t]}(h).\n\\]\nThe bounded support and continuity of \\(F\\) give \\(F(q_t)=t\\), so \\(\\rho(P_t)=t\\). The projections are nested and\n\\(d_\\rho(P_t,P_v)^2=|t-v|\\); hence \\(t\\mapsto P_t\\) is continuous and injective. They generate \\(Z\\) even if only rational parameters are retained. Indeed\n\\[\n 1_{(-\\infty,a]}(h)\n =\\bigvee_{\\substack{t\\in\\mathbb Q\\\\0<t<F(a)}} P_t .\n\\]\nThe right side is below the left and has the same \\(\\rho\\)-value \\(F(a)\\), by normality; faithfulness proves equality. The formula includes the cases \\(F(a)=0\\) and \\(F(a)=1\\). All spectral projections of the generator therefore belong to the generated algebra.\n\nWe need a sequence \\((\\chi_n)\\) of distinct invariants of states. In type I∞, take trace-class densities with the two positive eigenvalues\n\\(1/2+1/(n+2)\\) and \\(1/2-1/(n+2)\\), \\(n\\ge1\\); (OR6) separates their orbits. In type II∞ or III, the unit-mass slice of \\(P_\\theta\\) is convex and is exactly the state image by (OR4) and mass preservation. It cannot be a singleton: then the full cone would be a single ray, and Section 4's dense-span conclusion would make \\(Z_*\\), hence \\(Z\\), one-dimensional. Take two distinct points in that slice and their convex combinations with coefficients \\(1/(n+1)\\). These give the required sequence; every \\(\\chi_n\\) is realized by a state.\n\nChoose a countable norm base \\((B_j)\\) for the separable metric space \\(Z_*\\), using rational-radius balls about a countable dense set. Membership in these balls separates points. Let \\(a_j=1\\) when \\(j\\) is a power of two and \\(a_j=0\\) otherwise, and set\n\\[\n b(\\chi)=\\sum_{j\\ge1}\n \\left(3^{-(2j-1)}1_{B_j}(\\chi)+3^{-2j}a_j\\right).\n\\]\nThe series is uniformly convergent, so \\(b\\) is Borel. The first differing ternary digit dominates the remaining tail, hence \\(b\\) is injective. Its values lie in \\((0,1)\\) and are irrational. To verify the latter assertion, a rational number has an eventually periodic base-three expansion, by the finite-remainder long-division algorithm; the terminating expansion has the same property. Our expansion has only digits zero and one, and its even-position subsequence is \\((a_j)\\), which is not eventually periodic because it has infinitely many ones and unbounded gaps between them. Thus this expansion cannot represent a rational number.\n\nEnumerate the rational numbers in \\((0,1)\\) without repetition as \\((t_n)\\). Modify the code only at the selected invariants:\n\\[\n \\beta(\\chi)=\n \\begin{cases}\n t_n,&\\chi=\\chi_n,\\\\\n b(\\chi),&\\chi\\notin\\{\\chi_n:n\\ge1\\}.\n \\end{cases}\n\\]\nSingletons are norm closed, so the countable modification is Borel. It stays injective: the exceptional values are distinct rationals, and every other value is irrational. Now define\n\\[\n p(\\phi)=P_{\\beta(\\chi_\\phi)} .\n \\tag{OR11}\n\\]\nThis is Borel by the continuity of \\(\\phi\\mapsto\\chi_\\phi\\) and \\(t\\mapsto P_t\\). Injectivity of the two scalar codes and (OR2) prove exactly the equivalence in (OR10). The state image contains every \\(P_{t_n}\\), since every \\(\\chi_n\\) is realized. The rational-parameter argument proves that this image generates \\(Z\\).\n\n<a id=\"crng-scaling-extension\"><\/a>\n### Extend the state labels before applying scalar multiplication\n\nDefine a map on all positive normal functionals by\n\\[\n \\widehat p(0)=0,\\qquad\n \\widehat p(\\phi)=\n \\theta_{-\\log m}\\bigl(p(\\phi/m)\\bigr),\\qquad m=\\phi(1)>0.\n \\tag{OR12}\n\\]\nIt agrees with \\(p\\) on states. The map is Borel, including at zero. Mass and normalization are continuous on the punctured positive cone. For completeness the flow is jointly continuous on \\(\\mathbb R\\times\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\\): if \\(s_n\\to s\\) and \\(e_n\\to e\\) in \\(d_\\rho\\), then\n\\[\n \\rho\\theta_{s_n}(|e_n-e|^2)\n \\le\\|\\rho\\theta_{s_n}-\\rho\\theta_s\\|\n       +\\rho\\theta_s(|e_n-e|^2)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThe first term tends to zero by the predual norm-continuity proof in [CAPP's resolvent section](OA-FLOW-CAPP.md#capp-resolvent). For the second, write \\(\\rho\\theta_s=d_s\\rho\\), truncate \\(d_s\\), and use \\(0\\le|e_n-e|^2\\le1\\). Pointwise continuity of \\(s\\mapsto\\theta_s(e)\\), followed by the triangle inequality, proves joint continuity. Thus (OR12) is a composition of Borel maps on the punctured cone, with the Borel singleton zero handled separately.\n\nFor every real \\(s\\) and every positive normal \\(\\phi\\), direct substitution gives\n\\[\n \\widehat p(e^{-s}\\phi)=\\theta_s(\\widehat p(\\phi)).\n \\tag{OR13}\n\\]\nFor nonzero \\(\\phi\\), its normalized state is unchanged by this scaling, while its new mass is \\(e^{-s}m\\); the group parameter in (OR12) becomes \\(s-\\log m\\). For zero both sides are zero. This is the scalar-equivariant projection statement with a domain on which both sides are defined.\n\nOnly the state restriction was asserted to separate orbits by a projection alone. On the whole positive cone the pair\n\\((\\phi(1),\\widehat p(\\phi))\\) separates closed orbits: equal positive masses let us undo the same \\(\\theta_{-\\log m}\\) and apply the state result; mass zero forces both functionals to be zero. Conversely equal closed positive orbits have equal masses and equal normalized closed state orbits. In the scalar-center case, \\(\\widehat p\\) is simply one on every nonzero functional, illustrating why that additional mass coordinate cannot be discarded.\n\nA normal conjugacy of center flows transports these projections to any other normal flow chart. It preserves their equality and generated von Neumann algebra. It also preserves the bounded strong Borel structure: the defining normal-positive-functional seminorms pull back along the isomorphism and its normal inverse. Intertwining the two actions transports (OR13) with the same parameter and sign.\n\nThe human source is U. Haagerup and E. Størmer, [*Equivalence of Normal States on von Neumann Algebras and the Flow of Weights*](https://www.isibang.ac.in/~soumyashant/misc/collected-works-of-haagerup/1990s/1990_Equivalence_of_normal_states_on_von_Neumann_algebras_and_the_flow_of_weights.pdf), Advances in Mathematics 83 (1990), 180–262, especially §§3–8. This theorem retains the separable-predual infinite-factor scope; it does not assert the paper's further arbitrary-cardinality extension.\n","html":"<h1>The central functional classifies closed unitary orbits<\/h1>\n<p>The invariant is a positive normal functional on the center of the continuous core. It is not a central projection. Its mass retains the mass of the original functional, while the flow records scalar multiplication.<\/p>\n<p>Earlier complete proofs: <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CINV.html#cinv-mass\">CINV.MASS<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CINV.html#cinv-inverse\">CINV.INVERSE<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CINV.html#cinv-covariance\">CINV.COVARIANCE<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CINV.html#cinv-metric\">CINV.METRIC<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CSAS.html#csas-distance\">CSAS.DISTANCE<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CSAS.html#csas-range\">CSAS.RANGE<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CEXP.html#cexp-convergence\">CEXP.CONVERGENCE<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CAPP.html#capp-iii-zero\">CAPP.III-ZERO<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CPER.html#cper-masses\">CPER.MASSES<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CPER.html#cper-range\">CPER.RANGE<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MIV.html#miv-1\">MIV.1<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MIV.html#miv-2\">MIV.2<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-HC.html#oa-flow.hc.7\">HC.7<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SORB.html#orbit-type-i\">SORB.TYPE-I<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SORB.html#orbit-range\">SORB.RANGE<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CORE.html#core-9\">CORE.9<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CSAS.html#csas-type-i\">CSAS.TYPE-I<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CAPP.html#capp-resolvent\">CAPP.RESOLVENT<\/a>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SORB.html#orbit-diameters\">SORB.DIAMETERS<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2>1. The full theorem<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> be a nonzero infinite factor with separable predual. Let\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((C,\\tau,\\theta)\\)\">\\((C,\\tau,\\theta)\\)<\/span> be its common continuous core, with original Haar measure <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(dt\\)\">\\(dt\\)<\/span>, dual Haar measure <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(ds/(2\\pi)\\)\">\\(ds/(2\\pi)\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\theta_s=e^{-s}\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\theta_s=e^{-s}\\tau\\)<\/span>. Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z=Z(C)\\)\">\\(Z=Z(C)\\)<\/span>. For every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\in M_*^+\\)\">\\(\\phi\\in M_*^+\\)<\/span>, including zero and nonfaithful functionals, use SCW's supported dual density and define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n e_\\phi=1_{(1,\\infty)}(h_\\phi),\\qquad\n \\chi_\\phi(z)=2\\pi\\tau(e_\\phi z)\\quad(z\\in Z).                      \\tag{OR1}\n\\]\">\\[\n e_\\phi=1_{(1,\\infty)}(h_\\phi),\\qquad\n \\chi_\\phi(z)=2\\pi\\tau(e_\\phi z)\\quad(z\\in Z).                      \\tag{OR1}\n\\]<\/span>\nThen:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_\\phi\\)\">\\(e_\\phi\\)<\/span> has trace <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi(1)/(2\\pi)\\)\">\\(\\phi(1)/(2\\pi)\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_\\phi\\)\">\\(\\chi_\\phi\\)<\/span> is positive normal with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\chi_\\phi\\|=\\phi(1)\\)\">\\(\\|\\chi_\\phi\\|=\\phi(1)\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>Canonical normal chart identifications carry this functional to the same invariant; <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_{\\phi\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u}=\\chi_\\phi\\)\">\\(\\chi_{\\phi\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u}=\\chi_\\phi\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>For all positive normal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi,\\psi\\)\">\\(\\phi,\\psi\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n   \\boxed{\\quad\n   \\inf_{u\\in\\mathcal U(M)}\\|\\phi\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u-\\psi\\|\n          =\\|\\chi_\\phi-\\chi_\\psi\\| .\n   \\quad}                                                        \\tag{OR2}\n   \\]\">\\[\n   \\boxed{\\quad\n   \\inf_{u\\in\\mathcal U(M)}\\|\\phi\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u-\\psi\\|\n          =\\|\\chi_\\phi-\\chi_\\psi\\| .\n   \\quad}                                                        \\tag{OR2}\n   \\]<\/span><\/li>\n<li>The map is a contraction for the predual norms, hence norm-continuous and Borel. For every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c&gt;0\\)\">\\(c&gt;0\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n   \\chi_{c\\phi}=c\\,\\chi_\\phi\\circ\\theta_{\\log c}.                   \\tag{OR3}\n   \\]\">\\[\n   \\chi_{c\\phi}=c\\,\\chi_\\phi\\circ\\theta_{\\log c}.                   \\tag{OR3}\n   \\]<\/span>\nAt <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c=0\\)\">\\(c=0\\)<\/span> its value is zero.<\/li>\n<li>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> is type II∞ or type III, its exact image is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n   P_\\theta=\\{\\chi\\in Z_*^+:\n               \\chi\\theta_s\\ge e^{-s}\\chi\\text{ for every }s\\ge0\\}.\n                                                                    \\tag{OR4}\n   \\]\">\\[\n   P_\\theta=\\{\\chi\\in Z_*^+:\n               \\chi\\theta_s\\ge e^{-s}\\chi\\text{ for every }s\\ge0\\}.\n                                                                    \\tag{OR4}\n   \\]<\/span>\nFor type I∞ the exact image is the discrete tail range in Section 3 below.<\/li>\n<li>The family <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\{\\chi_\\phi:\\phi\\in M_*^+\\}\\)\">\\(\\{\\chi_\\phi:\\phi\\in M_*^+\\}\\)<\/span> separates <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>: an element of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span> annihilated by every member is zero.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Thus (OR1) induces an onto isometry from norm-closed unitary orbits onto the stated image. Equality of invariants is exactly equality of those closed orbits. Restricting to mass one gives the corresponding normal-state theorem. The domain must be the positive predual for (OR3); nontrivial scalar multiplication does not preserve the state space.<\/p>\n<h2>2. Proof of the distance and range in every type<\/h2>\n<p><a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CINV.html#cinv-mass\">The finite spectral-cut construction<\/a> proves the mass, chart covariance, inner invariance, scalar covariance, contraction and membership in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_\\theta\\)\">\\(P_\\theta\\)<\/span>, with the exact finite-domain pairings and full density domains. It also proves the metric on closed orbits is complete by a summable sequence of actual unitary alignments. Its support-sensitive partial-isometry approximation (CI18)–(CI19) retains zero complements and gives an explicit norm loss.<\/p>\n<p>For type II∞, the <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CSAS.html#csas-distance\">semifinite assembly<\/a>, including its <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CSAS.html#csas-range\">range construction<\/a>, applies, with a one-point base in the factor case. Equations (SA12) and (SA18) prove (OR2) and (OR4). Its nonfactor theorem is needed below and was proved separately by measurable near-minimizers and a jointly selected full trace flag.<\/p>\n<p>For type III<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(_\\lambda\\)\">\\(_\\lambda\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\)\">\\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\)<\/span>, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CPER.html#cper-comparison\">the compact-to-continuous core comparison<\/a> proves the normal spatial comparison between the compact and continuous cores, their exact trace scalar, the common supported densities and the actual central functional. Its unequal-mass extension (PCO13)–(PCO15) proves (OR2), and its full circle range (PCO16) proves (OR4). This use is a metric and range theorem; no diameter formula is substituted.<\/p>\n<p>For type III₀, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CEXP.html#cexp-convergence\">the finite-orbit-block construction<\/a> constructs increasing type II∞ subalgebras <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_i\\)\">\\(F_i\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> with faithful normal compatible expectations satisfying\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\eta E_i-\\eta\\|\\to0\\)\">\\(\\|\\eta E_i-\\eta\\|\\to0\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta\\in M_*\\)\">\\(\\eta\\in M_*\\)<\/span>.\nThey are generally nonfactors. Their full distance and range statements are (SA12) and (SA18). <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CAPP.html#capp-distance\">The core approximation argument<\/a> lifts these expectations to the cores, proves exact trace preservation and supported-density inclusion, then proves the varying-center restriction-norm limit. Its equations (CM11) and (CM15) give (OR2) and (OR4) for the original type III₀ factor.<\/p>\n<p>For completeness consider type III₁ explicitly. The actual modular-invariant theorem MIV1–2 identifies\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n S(M)\\cap(0,\\infty)=\\exp K,\\qquad\n K=\\ker(\\theta|_Z).\n\\]\">\\[\n S(M)\\cap(0,\\infty)=\\exp K,\\qquad\n K=\\ker(\\theta|_Z).\n\\]<\/span>\nIts normal center comparison and ergodicity are part of that theorem. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S(M)=[0,\\infty)\\)\">\\(S(M)=[0,\\infty)\\)<\/span>, injectivity of the real exponential gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K=\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(K=\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>. Thus the center action is trivial; ergodicity then gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z=\\mathbb C1\\)\">\\(Z=\\mathbb C1\\)<\/span>. Formula (OR1) is therefore the scalar functional of mass <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi(1)\\)\">\\(\\phi(1)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m=\\phi(1)\\)\">\\(m=\\phi(1)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n=\\psi(1)\\)\">\\(n=\\psi(1)\\)<\/span>. Testing on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span> gives the lower bound\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_M(\\phi,\\psi)\\ge|m-n|\\)\">\\(\\delta_M(\\phi,\\psi)\\ge|m-n|\\)<\/span>. If one mass is zero, the bound is equality by the positive-functional norm identity. Otherwise write\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi=m\\rho,\\ \\psi=n\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\phi=m\\rho,\\ \\psi=n\\sigma\\)<\/span>, with normal states <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho,\\sigma\\)\">\\(\\rho,\\sigma\\)<\/span>.\nThe full normal-state homogeneity theorem HC, including its nonfaithful approximation in HC7, gives unitaries with\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\rho\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u-\\sigma\\|\\to0\\)\">\\(\\|\\rho\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u-\\sigma\\|\\to0\\)<\/span>. Hence\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|\\phi\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u-\\psi\\|\n \\le m\\|\\rho\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u-\\sigma\\|+|m-n|\n \\longrightarrow |m-n|.\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|\\phi\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u-\\psi\\|\n \\le m\\|\\rho\\circ\\operatorname{Ad}u-\\sigma\\|+|m-n|\n \\longrightarrow |m-n|.\n\\]<\/span>\nThis proves\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\delta_M(\\phi,\\psi)=|m-n|=\\|\\chi_\\phi-\\chi_\\psi\\|.                 \\tag{OR5}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\delta_M(\\phi,\\psi)=|m-n|=\\|\\chi_\\phi-\\chi_\\psi\\|.                 \\tag{OR5}\n\\]<\/span>\nEvery nonnegative scalar is realized by a scalar multiple of one normal state. These are precisely the members of (OR4) on the scalar center.<\/p>\n<h2>3. The exact type I∞ range<\/h2>\n<p>Use the usual trace on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(H)\\)\">\\(B(H)\\)<\/span>, where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> is separable and infinite-dimensional. In the Fourier coordinate of the continuous core, the central action is\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_s(z)(q)=z(q-s)\\)\">\\(\\theta_s(z)(q)=z(q-s)\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2\\ge\\cdots\\ge0\\)\">\\(\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2\\ge\\cdots\\ge0\\)<\/span> are the positive eigenvalues of the trace-class density, repeated with multiplicity and padded by zeros as needed, then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n f_\\phi(a)=\\#\\{j:\\lambda_j&gt;a\\},\\qquad\n \\chi_\\phi(z)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}z(q)e^{-q}f_\\phi(e^{-q})\\,dq.         \\tag{OR6}\n\\]\">\\[\n f_\\phi(a)=\\#\\{j:\\lambda_j&gt;a\\},\\qquad\n \\chi_\\phi(z)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}z(q)e^{-q}f_\\phi(e^{-q})\\,dq.         \\tag{OR6}\n\\]<\/span>\nSORB's actual finite-eigenvector matching proof gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\delta_M(\\phi,\\psi)\n   =\\sum_j|\\lambda_j(\\phi)-\\lambda_j(\\psi)|\n   =\\int_0^\\infty|f_\\phi(a)-f_\\psi(a)|\\,da\n   =\\|\\chi_\\phi-\\chi_\\psi\\|.\n\\]\">\\[\n \\delta_M(\\phi,\\psi)\n   =\\sum_j|\\lambda_j(\\phi)-\\lambda_j(\\psi)|\n   =\\int_0^\\infty|f_\\phi(a)-f_\\psi(a)|\\,da\n   =\\|\\chi_\\phi-\\chi_\\psi\\|.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe exact range consists of decreasing right-continuous integrable\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb N_0\\)\">\\(f:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb N_0\\)<\/span> in (OR6). Conversely the eigenvalue formula\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_j=\\sup\\{a:f(a)\\ge j\\}\\)\">\\(\\lambda_j=\\sup\\{a:f(a)\\ge j\\}\\)<\/span> proves realization and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_j\\lambda_j=\\int f\\)\">\\(\\sum_j\\lambda_j=\\int f\\)<\/span>, as in SORB(SO26)–(SO27).\nIf the chosen semifinite trace is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\operatorname{Tr}\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\operatorname{Tr}\\)<\/span>, retain the corresponding value set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha\\mathbb N_0\\)\">\\(\\alpha\\mathbb N_0\\)<\/span> and its trace-relative density; canonical chart covariance identifies the resulting core-center functional.<\/p>\n<p>The continuous cone (OR4) has additional members. The profile\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(a)=\\tfrac12 1_{(0,2)}(a)\\)\">\\(f(a)=\\tfrac12 1_{(0,2)}(a)\\)<\/span> is decreasing, right-continuous, integrable with mass one, and gives a subinvariant functional by (OR6), but is not integer-valued. It is therefore absent from the type I∞ image. This is a range exception, not an exception to the isometry.<\/p>\n<h2>4. Separation of the entire center<\/h2>\n<p>First suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> is type II∞ or III. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CAPP.html#capp-resolvent\">The resolvent-cone argument<\/a>, (CM12)–(CM13), proves that the positive resolvent averages\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n R\\eta=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-t}\\eta\\theta_{-t}\\,dt,\\qquad\\eta\\in Z_*^+,\n\\]\">\\[\n R\\eta=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-t}\\eta\\theta_{-t}\\,dt,\\qquad\\eta\\in Z_*^+,\n\\]<\/span>\nbelong to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_\\theta\\)\">\\(P_\\theta\\)<\/span>, and that their norm closure is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_\\theta\\)\">\\(P_\\theta\\)<\/span>.\nLet <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in Z\\)\">\\(z\\in Z\\)<\/span> satisfy <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi(z)=0\\)\">\\(\\chi(z)=0\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi\\in P_\\theta\\)\">\\(\\chi\\in P_\\theta\\)<\/span>.\nSince the cone is invariant under every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_a\\)\">\\(\\theta_a\\)<\/span>, also\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((R\\eta)(\\theta_a z)=0\\)\">\\((R\\eta)(\\theta_a z)=0\\)<\/span> for all real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span>. Write\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h(a)=\\eta(\\theta_a z)\\)\">\\(h(a)=\\eta(\\theta_a z)\\)<\/span>; this is bounded and continuous. Changing variables gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n 0=e^a(R\\eta)(\\theta_a z)\n   =\\int_{-\\infty}^a e^r h(r)\\,dr\\qquad(a\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\]\">\\[\n 0=e^a(R\\eta)(\\theta_a z)\n   =\\int_{-\\infty}^a e^r h(r)\\,dr\\qquad(a\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\]<\/span>\nThe integrand is continuous and integrable at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-\\infty\\)\">\\(-\\infty\\)<\/span>, so differentiation yields <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^ah(a)=0\\)\">\\(e^ah(a)=0\\)<\/span>. In particular <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta(z)=0\\)\">\\(\\eta(z)=0\\)<\/span> for every positive normal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\eta\\)\">\\(\\eta\\)<\/span>. Positive normal functionals separate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span> (use vector functionals and polarization), hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=0\\)\">\\(z=0\\)<\/span>. Full range (OR4) proves the asserted separation by the image.<\/p>\n<p>For type I∞, use rank-one positive functionals of mass <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a&gt;0\\)\">\\(a&gt;0\\)<\/span>. Their profiles are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1_{(0,a)}\\)\">\\(1_{(0,a)}\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> annihilates the whole image, then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\int_t^\\infty e^{-q}z(q)\\,dq=0\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R),\n\\]\">\\[\n \\int_t^\\infty e^{-q}z(q)\\,dq=0\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R),\n\\]<\/span>\nby putting <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a=e^{-t}\\)\">\\(a=e^{-t}\\)<\/span>. Differences show that the integral of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-q}z(q)\\)\">\\(e^{-q}z(q)\\)<\/span> on every finite interval is zero. Its locally integrable density is therefore zero almost everywhere, for example by uniqueness of the scalar Radon–Nikodym derivative. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=0\\)\">\\(z=0\\)<\/span> in this case as well.<\/p>\n<p>Equivalently, the norm-closed complex linear span of the image is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z_*\\)\">\\(Z_*\\)<\/span>: if it were a proper closed subspace, Hahn–Banach and the concrete predual duality would supply a nonzero <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in Z\\)\">\\(z\\in Z\\)<\/span> annihilating it. This is a separation statement about normal functionals, not an algebra-generation assertion about them.<\/p>\n<h2>5. Exact consequences and projection coordinates<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The zero orbit maps to zero, and distance to that orbit is exactly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi(1)\\)\">\\(\\phi(1)\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c=e^{-s}&gt;0\\)\">\\(c=e^{-s}&gt;0\\)<\/span>, equation (OR3) reads\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_{e^{-s}\\phi}=e^{-s}\\chi_\\phi\\theta_{-s}\\)\">\\(\\chi_{e^{-s}\\phi}=e^{-s}\\chi_\\phi\\theta_{-s}\\)<\/span>, with both the mass factor and negative time retained.<\/li>\n<li>Equality of two invariants concerns closed orbits. It need not be attained by one unitary. Even on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B(\\ell^2)\\)\">\\(B(\\ell^2)\\)<\/span>, the densities with diagonal lists <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((2^{-1},2^{-2},\\ldots)\\)\">\\((2^{-1},2^{-2},\\ldots)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((0,2^{-1},2^{-2},\\ldots)\\)\">\\((0,2^{-1},2^{-2},\\ldots)\\)<\/span> have the same positive eigenvalues but different kernel dimensions.<\/li>\n<li>A scalar center has only two projections but has positive normal functionals of every nonnegative mass. The invariant in (OR1) therefore has the required codomain even in type III₁.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3>Central projections as countable coordinates<\/h3>\n<p>Fix a faithful normal state <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>. Such a state exists: the regular core embeds in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\bar\\otimes B(L^2(\\mathbb R))\\)\">\\(M\\bar\\otimes B(L^2(\\mathbb R))\\)<\/span>. The tensor-functional density argument in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CAPP.html#capp-lift\">CAPP, Section 2<\/a>, together with the separability of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_*\\)\">\\(M_*\\)<\/span> and of the trace-class operators on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(\\mathbb R)\\)\">\\(L^2(\\mathbb R)\\)<\/span>, makes the ambient predual separable. The normal restriction quotients in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.6\">CP6<\/a> give separability for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_*\\)\">\\(C_*\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z_*\\)\">\\(Z_*\\)<\/span>. The explicit faithful-state construction in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-HC.html#oa-flow.hc.7\">HC7<\/a> then applies.<\/p>\n<p>For the required separable Hilbert realization, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CR.html#oa-flow.cr.8\">CR8<\/a> sends a countable norm-dense set of positive normal functionals of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> to a dense set in its standard cone, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\xi_f-\\xi_g\\|^2\\le\\|f-g\\|\\)\">\\(\\|\\xi_f-\\xi_g\\|^2\\le\\|f-g\\|\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CR.html#oa-flow.cr.1\">CR1<\/a> proves that the complex span of that cone is the full standard Hilbert space. It is therefore separable, as is the regular core space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(\\mathbb R,H)\\)\">\\(L^2(\\mathbb R,H)\\)<\/span>. In particular its represented center satisfies DC06's separable-Hilbert-space hypothesis.<\/p>\n<p>Use the abelian realization in <a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DC.html#realizing-an-abelian-algebra-as-the-diagonal-algebra\">DC06<\/a> and the finite-measure density proof in <a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DC.html#a-finite-radon-nikodym-density-from-hilbert-representation\">DC05<\/a>. They give a unique nonnegative <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_\\phi\\in L^1(Z,\\rho)\\)\">\\(g_\\phi\\in L^1(Z,\\rho)\\)<\/span> with\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\chi_\\phi(z)=\\rho(g_\\phi z),\\qquad\n \\rho(g_\\phi)=\\phi(1),\\qquad\n \\|\\chi_\\phi-\\chi_\\psi\\|=\\rho(|g_\\phi-g_\\psi|).\n \\tag{OR7}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\chi_\\phi(z)=\\rho(g_\\phi z),\\qquad\n \\rho(g_\\phi)=\\phi(1),\\qquad\n \\|\\chi_\\phi-\\chi_\\psi\\|=\\rho(|g_\\phi-g_\\psi|).\n \\tag{OR7}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe last equality follows by testing the bounded sign of the real density difference; the reverse inequality is the integral triangle inequality. Faithfulness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> identifies its null sets with the null projections of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For every positive rational <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>, define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n p_{\\phi,r}=1_{(r,\\infty)}(g_\\phi)\\in\\operatorname{Proj}(Z),\\qquad\n \\mathbf p_\\rho(\\phi)=(p_{\\phi,r})_{r\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}}.\n \\tag{OR8}\n\\]\">\\[\n p_{\\phi,r}=1_{(r,\\infty)}(g_\\phi)\\in\\operatorname{Proj}(Z),\\qquad\n \\mathbf p_\\rho(\\phi)=(p_{\\phi,r})_{r\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}}.\n \\tag{OR8}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe densities are finite almost everywhere, so their rational strict superlevel sets determine them. Explicitly, outside the union of the countably many null sets where two corresponding cuts differ, distinct finite density values would have a positive rational strictly between them. Thus the coordinate families coincide exactly when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_\\phi=g_\\psi\\)\">\\(g_\\phi=g_\\psi\\)<\/span>, equivalently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_\\phi=\\chi_\\psi\\)\">\\(\\chi_\\phi=\\chi_\\psi\\)<\/span>, equivalently the norm-closed unitary orbits coincide by (OR2). This includes zero and all nonfaithful functionals.<\/p>\n<p>Represent <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(Z,\\rho)\\)\">\\(L^2(Z,\\rho)\\)<\/span>, and give its projection space the strong-operator Borel structure, metrized by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_\\rho(p,q)=\\rho(|p-q|^2)^{1/2}\\)\">\\(d_\\rho(p,q)=\\rho(|p-q|^2)^{1/2}\\)<\/span>. On uniformly bounded multiplication operators, convergence in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(\\rho)\\)\">\\(L^2(\\rho)\\)<\/span> implies strong convergence: truncate any test vector to a bounded vector, use the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2\\)\">\\(L^2\\)<\/span> bound on that part, then use the common operator bound on the remaining vector tail. Conversely strong convergence tested on the constant vector gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2\\)\">\\(L^2\\)<\/span> convergence. This also proves the asserted description on the projection space.<\/p>\n<p>Each map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\mapsto p_{\\phi,r}\\)\">\\(\\phi\\mapsto p_{\\phi,r}\\)<\/span> is Borel. Indeed (OR7) and contraction of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi\\)\">\\(\\chi\\)<\/span> make <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\mapsto g_\\phi\\)\">\\(\\phi\\mapsto g_\\phi\\)<\/span> continuous from the positive predual norm into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^1(\\rho)\\)\">\\(L^1(\\rho)\\)<\/span>. Put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n F_{n,r}(t)=\\min(1,n(t-r)_+).\n\\]\">\\[\n F_{n,r}(t)=\\min(1,n(t-r)_+).\n\\]<\/span>\nFor nonnegative <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g,h\\)\">\\(g,h\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|F_{n,r}(g)-F_{n,r}(h)\\|_2^2\\le n\\|g-h\\|_1\\)\">\\(\\|F_{n,r}(g)-F_{n,r}(h)\\|_2^2\\le n\\|g-h\\|_1\\)<\/span>,\nsince the function has Lipschitz constant <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span> and its values lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([0,1]\\)\">\\([0,1]\\)<\/span>. Hence these bounded-operator maps are continuous. Dominated convergence gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F_{n,r}(g)\\to1_{(r,\\infty)}(g)\\)\">\\(F_{n,r}(g)\\to1_{(r,\\infty)}(g)\\)<\/span> strongly. A pointwise limit of Borel maps into the metric space of positive contractions with metric <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_\\rho\\)\">\\(d_\\rho\\)<\/span> is Borel: distance to every closed set is the limit of the corresponding distances, and open sets can be tested by positive distance to their complements. The countable product map in (OR8) is consequently Borel.<\/p>\n<p>The coordinate projections from the <strong>full positive predual<\/strong> generate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span> be the von Neumann algebra they generate. All <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_\\phi\\)\">\\(g_\\phi\\)<\/span> are measurable for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>, because their rational spectral cuts lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>. Section 4 proves that the complex linear span of the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_\\phi\\)\">\\(\\chi_\\phi\\)<\/span> is norm dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z_*\\)\">\\(Z_*\\)<\/span>; by (OR7), the same density holds for the span of the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_\\phi\\)\">\\(g_\\phi\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^1(\\rho)\\)\">\\(L^1(\\rho)\\)<\/span>. Given a selfadjoint <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in Z\\)\">\\(z\\in Z\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|z\\|\\le K\\)\">\\(\\|z\\|\\le K\\)<\/span>, approximate it in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^1\\)\">\\(L^1\\)<\/span> by real linear combinations of the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_\\phi\\)\">\\(g_\\phi\\)<\/span>, taking real parts of complex approximations. Clip those combinations to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([-K,K]\\)\">\\([-K,K]\\)<\/span>. The clipped functions belong to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>, and clipping cannot increase their pointwise distance from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span>. Thus they converge to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\)\">\\(z\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^1\\)\">\\(L^1\\)<\/span>, hence in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2\\)\">\\(L^2\\)<\/span> by the common bound, and strongly by the preceding vector-tail argument. Strong closedness gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in B\\)\">\\(z\\in B\\)<\/span>, proving <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=Z\\)\">\\(B=Z\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<h3>Scalar covariance and the role of the domain<\/h3>\n<p>For real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_s=d(\\rho\\theta_s)/d\\rho\\)\">\\(d_s=d(\\rho\\theta_s)/d\\rho\\)<\/span>. It is positive and finite almost everywhere, because both states are faithful. For a nonnegative density <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span>, first use bounded cutoffs and then monotone convergence to obtain\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (g\\rho)\\theta_s(z)=\\rho\\bigl(d_s\\,\\theta_{-s}(g)\\,z\\bigr)\n \\qquad(z\\in Z_+).\n\\]\">\\[\n (g\\rho)\\theta_s(z)=\\rho\\bigl(d_s\\,\\theta_{-s}(g)\\,z\\bigr)\n \\qquad(z\\in Z_+).\n\\]<\/span>\nIndeed the right side is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\rho\\theta_s)(\\theta_{-s}(g)z)=\\rho(g\\theta_s(z))\\)\">\\((\\rho\\theta_s)(\\theta_{-s}(g)z)=\\rho(g\\theta_s(z))\\)<\/span>. Formula (OR3) therefore becomes\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n g_{c\\phi}=c\\,d_{\\log c}\\,\\theta_{-\\log c}(g_\\phi),\\qquad\n p_{c\\phi,r}=1_{(r,\\infty)}\n       \\bigl(c\\,d_{\\log c}\\,\\theta_{-\\log c}(g_\\phi)\\bigr)\n \\quad(c&gt;0).\n \\tag{OR9}\n\\]\">\\[\n g_{c\\phi}=c\\,d_{\\log c}\\,\\theta_{-\\log c}(g_\\phi),\\qquad\n p_{c\\phi,r}=1_{(r,\\infty)}\n       \\bigl(c\\,d_{\\log c}\\,\\theta_{-\\log c}(g_\\phi)\\bigr)\n \\quad(c&gt;0).\n \\tag{OR9}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe products are products of commuting measurable functions, and the displayed integral proves their integrability. At <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c=0\\)\">\\(c=0\\)<\/span> every coordinate is zero. For a state <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\)\">\\(\\phi\\)<\/span>, the functional <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-s}\\phi\\)\">\\(e^{-s}\\phi\\)<\/span> has mass <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-s}\\)\">\\(e^{-s}\\)<\/span>, so it belongs to the state space only when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s=0\\)\">\\(s=0\\)<\/span>. The positive-predual domain and the density factor in (OR9) are therefore part of the statement.<\/p>\n<p>State inputs alone need not generate the center by these coordinates. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=B(\\ell^2)\\)\">\\(M=B(\\ell^2)\\)<\/span>, a trace-class state density has every eigenvalue at most one. Formula (OR6) makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_\\phi\\)\">\\(\\chi_\\phi\\)<\/span>, and hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g_\\phi\\)\">\\(g_\\phi\\)<\/span>, vanish on the half-line <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q\\le0\\)\">\\(q\\le0\\)<\/span>. All its positive spectral cuts vanish there. Their generated unital algebra restricts to scalars on that half-line, whereas <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^\\infty(\\mathbb R)\\)\">\\(L^\\infty(\\mathbb R)\\)<\/span> contains nonconstant functions supported there. Thus the generation theorem just proved genuinely requires the full positive domain.<\/p>\n<p>The single spectral projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_\\phi\\)\">\\(e_\\phi\\)<\/span> of (OR1) is a different object: it lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span> and need not be central or constant on a unitary orbit. In the same type I example, the vector state at a basis vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_j\\)\">\\(e_j\\)<\/span> has\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_\\phi=|e_j\\rangle\\langle e_j|\\otimes1_{(0,\\infty)}(q)\\)\">\\(e_\\phi=|e_j\\rangle\\langle e_j|\\otimes1_{(0,\\infty)}(q)\\)<\/span>.\nMatrix units show this projection is noncentral; exchanging two basis vectors changes it within one state orbit. The central functional in (OR1) is unchanged.<\/p>\n<p>Finally, no map into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\\)<\/span> alone can separate all positive-functional orbits for every infinite factor. In type III₁, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z=\\mathbb C\\)\">\\(Z=\\mathbb C\\)<\/span> has only two projections, while (OR5) gives distinct orbits for any three distinct positive masses. The family (OR8) has the stated separation property. Its countable-coordinate assertion is distinct from a single-projection coding of the mass-one orbit space.<\/p>\n<h3>One central projection for each closed state orbit<\/h3>\n<p>There does exist a Borel map\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n p:S_*(M)\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\n \\tag{OR10}\n\\]\">\\[\n p:S_*(M)\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\n \\tag{OR10}\n\\]<\/span>\nsuch that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p(\\phi)=p(\\psi)\\)\">\\(p(\\phi)=p(\\psi)\\)<\/span> exactly when the closed state orbits coincide, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p(S_*(M))\\)\">\\(p(S_*(M))\\)<\/span> generates <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>. The following explicit coding proves existence. It is a choice of labels; its projections need not be the spectral projections in (OR1) or (OR8).<\/p>\n<p>First <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span> is either scalar or diffuse. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CORE.html#core-8\">CORE8<\/a> proves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z^\\theta=\\mathbb C1\\)\">\\(Z^\\theta=\\mathbb C1\\)<\/span> because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> is a factor. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\)\">\\(e\\)<\/span> is a nonzero minimal projection of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>, each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_s(e)\\)\">\\(\\theta_s(e)\\)<\/span> is either <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\)\">\\(e\\)<\/span> or is orthogonal to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e\\)\">\\(e\\)<\/span>. In the latter case\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(|\\theta_s(e)-e|^2)\\ge\\rho(e)&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\rho(|\\theta_s(e)-e|^2)\\ge\\rho(e)&gt;0\\)<\/span>.\nContinuity makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_s(e)=e\\)\">\\(\\theta_s(e)=e\\)<\/span> for all sufficiently small <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>. Dividing any real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> into sufficiently small equal increments proves invariance for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>. Ergodicity gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e=1\\)\">\\(e=1\\)<\/span>, and minimality of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span> means <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z=\\mathbb C1\\)\">\\(Z=\\mathbb C1\\)<\/span>. In this scalar case (OR2) makes all states have the same closed orbit, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p(\\phi)=1\\)\">\\(p(\\phi)=1\\)<\/span> proves (OR10) and generation.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose now <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span> is diffuse. DC06 supplies a bounded positive selfadjoint generator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>. Its spectral probability measure\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu(E)=\\rho(1_E(h))\\)\">\\(\\mu(E)=\\rho(1_E(h))\\)<\/span> has no atoms: a nonzero projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1_{\\{a\\}}(h)\\)\">\\(1_{\\{a\\}}(h)\\)<\/span> would be minimal in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z=\\{h\\}&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(Z=\\{h\\}&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>. Thus the distribution\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(a)=\\mu((-\\infty,a])\\)\">\\(F(a)=\\mu((-\\infty,a])\\)<\/span> is continuous. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\)\">\\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\)<\/span>, put\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n q_t=\\inf\\{a:F(a)\\ge t\\},\\qquad P_t=1_{(-\\infty,q_t]}(h).\n\\]\">\\[\n q_t=\\inf\\{a:F(a)\\ge t\\},\\qquad P_t=1_{(-\\infty,q_t]}(h).\n\\]<\/span>\nThe bounded support and continuity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(q_t)=t\\)\">\\(F(q_t)=t\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho(P_t)=t\\)\">\\(\\rho(P_t)=t\\)<\/span>. The projections are nested and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_\\rho(P_t,P_v)^2=|t-v|\\)\">\\(d_\\rho(P_t,P_v)^2=|t-v|\\)<\/span>; hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\mapsto P_t\\)\">\\(t\\mapsto P_t\\)<\/span> is continuous and injective. They generate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span> even if only rational parameters are retained. Indeed\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n 1_{(-\\infty,a]}(h)\n =\\bigvee_{\\substack{t\\in\\mathbb Q\\\\0&lt;t&lt;F(a)}} P_t .\n\\]\">\\[\n 1_{(-\\infty,a]}(h)\n =\\bigvee_{\\substack{t\\in\\mathbb Q\\\\0&lt;t&lt;F(a)}} P_t .\n\\]<\/span>\nThe right side is below the left and has the same <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span>-value <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(a)\\)\">\\(F(a)\\)<\/span>, by normality; faithfulness proves equality. The formula includes the cases <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(a)=0\\)\">\\(F(a)=0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F(a)=1\\)\">\\(F(a)=1\\)<\/span>. All spectral projections of the generator therefore belong to the generated algebra.<\/p>\n<p>We need a sequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\chi_n)\\)\">\\((\\chi_n)\\)<\/span> of distinct invariants of states. In type I∞, take trace-class densities with the two positive eigenvalues\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/2+1/(n+2)\\)\">\\(1/2+1/(n+2)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/2-1/(n+2)\\)\">\\(1/2-1/(n+2)\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\ge1\\)\">\\(n\\ge1\\)<\/span>; (OR6) separates their orbits. In type II∞ or III, the unit-mass slice of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_\\theta\\)\">\\(P_\\theta\\)<\/span> is convex and is exactly the state image by (OR4) and mass preservation. It cannot be a singleton: then the full cone would be a single ray, and Section 4's dense-span conclusion would make <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z_*\\)\">\\(Z_*\\)<\/span>, hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>, one-dimensional. Take two distinct points in that slice and their convex combinations with coefficients <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1/(n+1)\\)\">\\(1/(n+1)\\)<\/span>. These give the required sequence; every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_n\\)\">\\(\\chi_n\\)<\/span> is realized by a state.<\/p>\n<p>Choose a countable norm base <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((B_j)\\)\">\\((B_j)\\)<\/span> for the separable metric space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z_*\\)\">\\(Z_*\\)<\/span>, using rational-radius balls about a countable dense set. Membership in these balls separates points. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_j=1\\)\">\\(a_j=1\\)<\/span> when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(j\\)\">\\(j\\)<\/span> is a power of two and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_j=0\\)\">\\(a_j=0\\)<\/span> otherwise, and set\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n b(\\chi)=\\sum_{j\\ge1}\n \\left(3^{-(2j-1)}1_{B_j}(\\chi)+3^{-2j}a_j\\right).\n\\]\">\\[\n b(\\chi)=\\sum_{j\\ge1}\n \\left(3^{-(2j-1)}1_{B_j}(\\chi)+3^{-2j}a_j\\right).\n\\]<\/span>\nThe series is uniformly convergent, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span> is Borel. The first differing ternary digit dominates the remaining tail, hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span> is injective. Its values lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((0,1)\\)\">\\((0,1)\\)<\/span> and are irrational. To verify the latter assertion, a rational number has an eventually periodic base-three expansion, by the finite-remainder long-division algorithm; the terminating expansion has the same property. Our expansion has only digits zero and one, and its even-position subsequence is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((a_j)\\)\">\\((a_j)\\)<\/span>, which is not eventually periodic because it has infinitely many ones and unbounded gaps between them. Thus this expansion cannot represent a rational number.<\/p>\n<p>Enumerate the rational numbers in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((0,1)\\)\">\\((0,1)\\)<\/span> without repetition as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((t_n)\\)\">\\((t_n)\\)<\/span>. Modify the code only at the selected invariants:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\beta(\\chi)=\n \\begin{cases}\n t_n,&amp;\\chi=\\chi_n,\\\\\n b(\\chi),&amp;\\chi\\notin\\{\\chi_n:n\\ge1\\}.\n \\end{cases}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\beta(\\chi)=\n \\begin{cases}\n t_n,&amp;\\chi=\\chi_n,\\\\\n b(\\chi),&amp;\\chi\\notin\\{\\chi_n:n\\ge1\\}.\n \\end{cases}\n\\]<\/span>\nSingletons are norm closed, so the countable modification is Borel. It stays injective: the exceptional values are distinct rationals, and every other value is irrational. Now define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n p(\\phi)=P_{\\beta(\\chi_\\phi)} .\n \\tag{OR11}\n\\]\">\\[\n p(\\phi)=P_{\\beta(\\chi_\\phi)} .\n \\tag{OR11}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis is Borel by the continuity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\mapsto\\chi_\\phi\\)\">\\(\\phi\\mapsto\\chi_\\phi\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\mapsto P_t\\)\">\\(t\\mapsto P_t\\)<\/span>. Injectivity of the two scalar codes and (OR2) prove exactly the equivalence in (OR10). The state image contains every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_{t_n}\\)\">\\(P_{t_n}\\)<\/span>, since every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_n\\)\">\\(\\chi_n\\)<\/span> is realized. The rational-parameter argument proves that this image generates <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Z\\)\">\\(Z\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<h3>Extend the state labels before applying scalar multiplication<\/h3>\n<p>Define a map on all positive normal functionals by\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\widehat p(0)=0,\\qquad\n \\widehat p(\\phi)=\n \\theta_{-\\log m}\\bigl(p(\\phi/m)\\bigr),\\qquad m=\\phi(1)&gt;0.\n \\tag{OR12}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\widehat p(0)=0,\\qquad\n \\widehat p(\\phi)=\n \\theta_{-\\log m}\\bigl(p(\\phi/m)\\bigr),\\qquad m=\\phi(1)&gt;0.\n \\tag{OR12}\n\\]<\/span>\nIt agrees with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> on states. The map is Borel, including at zero. Mass and normalization are continuous on the punctured positive cone. For completeness the flow is jointly continuous on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb R\\times\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\\)\">\\(\\mathbb R\\times\\operatorname{Proj}(Z)\\)<\/span>: if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s_n\\to s\\)\">\\(s_n\\to s\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_n\\to e\\)\">\\(e_n\\to e\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_\\rho\\)\">\\(d_\\rho\\)<\/span>, then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\rho\\theta_{s_n}(|e_n-e|^2)\n \\le\\|\\rho\\theta_{s_n}-\\rho\\theta_s\\|\n       +\\rho\\theta_s(|e_n-e|^2)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\">\\[\n \\rho\\theta_{s_n}(|e_n-e|^2)\n \\le\\|\\rho\\theta_{s_n}-\\rho\\theta_s\\|\n       +\\rho\\theta_s(|e_n-e|^2)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe first term tends to zero by the predual norm-continuity proof in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CAPP.html#capp-resolvent\">CAPP's resolvent section<\/a>. For the second, write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\theta_s=d_s\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\theta_s=d_s\\rho\\)<\/span>, truncate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_s\\)\">\\(d_s\\)<\/span>, and use <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\le|e_n-e|^2\\le1\\)\">\\(0\\le|e_n-e|^2\\le1\\)<\/span>. Pointwise continuity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\mapsto\\theta_s(e)\\)\">\\(s\\mapsto\\theta_s(e)\\)<\/span>, followed by the triangle inequality, proves joint continuity. Thus (OR12) is a composition of Borel maps on the punctured cone, with the Borel singleton zero handled separately.<\/p>\n<p>For every real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span> and every positive normal <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\)\">\\(\\phi\\)<\/span>, direct substitution gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\widehat p(e^{-s}\\phi)=\\theta_s(\\widehat p(\\phi)).\n \\tag{OR13}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\widehat p(e^{-s}\\phi)=\\theta_s(\\widehat p(\\phi)).\n \\tag{OR13}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor nonzero <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\)\">\\(\\phi\\)<\/span>, its normalized state is unchanged by this scaling, while its new mass is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-s}m\\)\">\\(e^{-s}m\\)<\/span>; the group parameter in (OR12) becomes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s-\\log m\\)\">\\(s-\\log m\\)<\/span>. For zero both sides are zero. This is the scalar-equivariant projection statement with a domain on which both sides are defined.<\/p>\n<p>Only the state restriction was asserted to separate orbits by a projection alone. On the whole positive cone the pair\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\phi(1),\\widehat p(\\phi))\\)\">\\((\\phi(1),\\widehat p(\\phi))\\)<\/span> separates closed orbits: equal positive masses let us undo the same <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta_{-\\log m}\\)\">\\(\\theta_{-\\log m}\\)<\/span> and apply the state result; mass zero forces both functionals to be zero. Conversely equal closed positive orbits have equal masses and equal normalized closed state orbits. In the scalar-center case, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widehat p\\)\">\\(\\widehat p\\)<\/span> is simply one on every nonzero functional, illustrating why that additional mass coordinate cannot be discarded.<\/p>\n<p>A normal conjugacy of center flows transports these projections to any other normal flow chart. It preserves their equality and generated von Neumann algebra. It also preserves the bounded strong Borel structure: the defining normal-positive-functional seminorms pull back along the isomorphism and its normal inverse. Intertwining the two actions transports (OR13) with the same parameter and sign.<\/p>\n<p>The human source is U. Haagerup and E. Størmer, <a href=\"https://www.isibang.ac.in/~soumyashant/misc/collected-works-of-haagerup/1990s/1990_Equivalence_of_normal_states_on_von_Neumann_algebras_and_the_flow_of_weights.pdf\"><em>Equivalence of Normal States on von Neumann Algebras and the Flow of Weights<\/em><\/a>, Advances in Mathematics 83 (1990), 180–262, especially §§3–8. This theorem retains the separable-predual infinite-factor scope; it does not assert the paper's further arbitrary-cardinality extension.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-CRNG.md","start_line":1,"end_line":286,"recorded_range_hash_matched":false}});
